<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<rdf:RDF
   xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
   xmlns:foaf="http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/"
   xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
   xmlns:plato="https://w3id.org/plato#"
   xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
   xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
   xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
   xmlns:vann="http://purl.org/vocab/vann/"
>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#transcription_accuracy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">transcription accuracy</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        How faithfully a transcribed value (a name form, most often) renders
        what the source shows: a concept in plato:TranscriptionAccuracyScheme,
        plato:TranscriptionAccurate, plato:TranscriptionInaccurate or
        plato:TranscriptionFalse. It judges the reading, not the source and not
        the identification: plato:form_status says how a form came to be, and
        plato:certainty how sure anyone is of the attestation, and neither says
        how well the form was read. Pleiades records it for every name.

        Like the other qualification properties it has no rdfs:domain, so a
        transcribed coordinate or date can be judged the same way.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#universe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">universe</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The figure this figure is a part or share of: its denominator, where
        the source defines one. Asserted only from the source, never inferred
        from labels or sums: in Vision of Ireland three of seven plausible
        denominators by name are wrong. The occupied beside the
        total of the same row, or Vision of Ireland's Total Population cube as
        the universe of the others for the same unit and year. RDF Data Cube
        has no such property, and without it which number is the denominator
        of which is lost, unrecoverably once the table is gone.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#meta_attestation_about">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">meta-attestation about</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links a meta-Attestation to the Attestation it comments on or relates to.
        Used with plato:has_meta_type to specify the nature of the relationship.

        Example: A meta-attestation might assert that one source's geometry
        attestation contradicts another source's geometry attestation for the
        same SpatialEntity.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_object">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identity object</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The second SpatialEntity in an identity assertion. Often a record in
        another authority, such as a GeoNames feature or a Wikidata item,
        which this range makes a SpatialEntity. That is intended: in PLATO
        an authority record is a SpatialEntity like any other, identified by
        its own URI, with its origin recorded in plato:namespace.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Source">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Source</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A citable source of evidence: a manuscript, chronicle, tax record,
        map, scholarly article, or other document. Sources are the primary
        provenance anchor for attestations. A Source may reference its parent
        Dataset via plato:partOf.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relative_distance">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">distance</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Distance from the anchor, in metres. Primarily used for
        relative geometries. May carry its own certainty or fuzziness.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#end_precision">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">end precision</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Temporal granularity of the end bound. Values: 'year', 'decade',
        'century', 'era', 'geological_period'.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#authority_uri">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">authority URI</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#UpstreamOf">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">upstream of</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Upstream of the anchor along a watercourse.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#entity_identifier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identifier</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        An identifier for this SpatialEntity other than its IRI: the
        contributing project's own record id ('GLOB_366') or an authority's
        ('geonames:2654675'), with plato:namespace saying whose. A finding aid,
        like a label: not a claim about the place.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#value_literal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">value</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The value, where it is a literal: a number, a string, a date. Use
        plato:value_json instead where the value is structured.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Literal"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#BetweenXAndY">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">between</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Between the anchor and a second anchor.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identity type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The semantic strength of the identity assertion:
        'exactMatch'  — the two SpatialEntities are unambiguously the same entity.
        'closeMatch'  — the two SpatialEntities are believed to be the same entity
                        but with some residual ambiguity (e.g. differing
                        granularity, overlapping but not identical scope).
        'related'     — the two SpatialEntities are closely associated but may not
                        be strictly identical (e.g. a settlement and its
                        administrative district, or successive phases of
                        a site that might or might not constitute continuity).
        'unspecified' — the source links the two but does not say how strongly;
                        honest where the alternative is to guess a strength.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionFalse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionAccuracyScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">false</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The transcribed form is not in the source at all: a ghost form, kept because it circulates and must be recognisable.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b10">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b11"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_email">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">email</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Contact email address for the contributor. Useful for collaboration
        and correspondence about contributed data.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Supersedes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">supersedes</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Replaces the target attestation with newer information.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#value_type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">value type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        URI declaring the shape of a structured value, so that a consumer can
        tell whether it understands the value before attempting to read it.
        Omitted for literal values.

        Not every attribute a source states is scalar. A market's trading day
        — 'the Monday after Whitsun, for three days' — is a recurrence rule
        anchored to a moveable feast, and flattening it to a string would
        reproduce the loss this class exists to prevent. The ontology stays
        agnostic about such shapes: it records which shape is in use and
        leaves the definition to the vocabulary that owns it.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#ccodes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">country codes</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 codes of the modern countries whose territory
        contains or overlaps this SpatialEntity, as in Linked Places Format.
        A finding aid for search and matching, like a label: not evidence,
        and not a claim about the past, so it needs no source. Where a
        country is itself the subject of a historical claim ("this town lay
        in the Kingdom of Aragon"), record it as a relation attestation.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#FormStatusScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">form status</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:form_status. The status of the name form an attestation contributes.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#has_relation_type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has relation type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Specifies the semantic type of a entity-to-entity relationship
        attested by an Attestation. References a RelationType authority
        which defines the relationship's label, inverse, and domain/range
        constraints. Examples: 'capital_of', 'successor_to',
        'connected_by_trade_route_to'.

        The extensible vocabulary pattern allows new relationship types to
        be added as Authority records without modifying the ontology.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#name_embedding">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">name embedding</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Vector embedding for phonetic similarity search. Typically a
        256-dimensional vector generated from the name's phonetic form,
        enabling fuzzy matching and transliteration-variant detection.
        Format is implementation-specific but should be documented in
        gazetteer metadata. WHG v4 uses Symphonym embeddings.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#source_timespan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">source timespan</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        When this Source, as a document, was produced: the date of the
        manuscript, the compilation of the cartulary, the publication of the
        edition. Distinct from the Timespan an attestation asserts, which
        dates the claim rather than the witness. An annal for 921 read in a
        manuscript written c. 925 has an attested timespan of 921 and a
        source timespan of c. 925. Reuses Timespan, so an approximate date
        is start_earliest 915, end_latest 935, timespan_label 'c. 925'. The
        same move as plato:has_timespan on a Period.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Source"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#InPersonalName">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#OccurrenceContextScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">in personal name</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The form occurs only as an element of a personal name, a surname or by-name; the (p) of the place-name surveys.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#attests_geometry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attests geometry</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to a Geometry it asserts for the SpatialEntity. An
        attestation may assert multiple geometries (e.g., point and polygon)
        or no geometry at all (e.g., name-only attestations from textual sources).
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relation_domain">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">domain constraint</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Valid subject type for this relation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A classification or category for a SpatialEntity, typically drawn from a
        controlled vocabulary such as the Getty Art &amp; Architecture Thesaurus
        (AAT). Types are independent, reusable entities.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#bbox">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bounding box</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Bounding box as array: [min_lon, min_lat, max_lon, max_lat].
        Enables fast spatial filtering. Format: comma-separated string
        or JSON array depending on serialization context.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#form_status">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">form status</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The status of the name form this attestation contributes: read in the
        cited source, chosen by the cited authority, or derived by the
        contributing project. Should point to a concept in a SKOS concept
        scheme. The starter concepts are:

        - plato:Attested: a form read in the cited source. The default, and
          usually omitted.
        - plato:Headword: the editorial or preferred form under which the
          cited authority files the entry ('Bunsty Hundred'). A scholarly
          decision rather than a reading, and typically the form users search
          for. Another authority may choose differently, which is why it is a
          sourced attestation and not a label on the SpatialEntity.
        - plato:Normalised: a form derived from an attested one for matching
          or search ('Bunstow(e)' resolved to 'Bunstowe'), attested by no
          source and carrying no independent evidential value. It may point
          to the attestation it was derived from through a meta-attestation.
        - plato:Reconstructed: a form inferred but not attested, the
          philologists' asterisked form.
        - plato:Preferred: the form the contributing project prefers for
          display and search ('Ranny (Fort)'). A project decision, so the cited
          source may show a different form.

        A different axis from plato:name_type, which classifies what the name
        denotes: a headword is still a toponym. It sits on the Attestation,
        not on the Name, because the same string can be a reading in one
        attestation and a headword in another. The WHG ingestion prototype
        reached the same placement independently: its preferred-name flag is
        on the attestation, not on the toponym record.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#type_label">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">type label</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Human-readable label for this type.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Type"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#candidate_status">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">status</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Review status: 'suggested', 'confirmed', 'rejected'.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#certainty_note">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">certainty note</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Free-text explanation of the certainty assessment: quality of
        evidence, degree of scholarly consensus, reliability of source
        (e.g. 'manuscript partially illegible', 'coordinates extrapolated
        from itinerary distances'). Applies wherever certainty does.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_subject">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identity subject</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The first SpatialEntity in an identity assertion.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#hull">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">convex hull</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Convex hull of the geometry as WKT Polygon. Used for quick spatial
        filtering before more expensive exact geometry operations. Optional
        but recommended for complex geometries.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#spatial_precision">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">spatial precision</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Precision qualifier for this geometry: 'exact' (surveyed or GPS),
        'approximate' (estimated from maps or descriptions), 'uncertain'
        (multiple possible locations), 'historical_approximate' (based on
        historical sources with inherent imprecision). Multiple values
        possible for heterogeneous geometries.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#value_unit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">value unit</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        URI of the unit of measurement, where the value has one — a QUDT unit
        or a Wikidata unit item. Omitted for counts and dimensionless values.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Direct">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#OccurrenceContextScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">direct</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The form refers to the SpatialEntity directly. The default occurrence context, usually omitted.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#has_citation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has citation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to a Citation: its qualified use of one
        Authority, with a locator and an attribution status. An attestation
        may have several, one per source it rests on.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Citation"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#has_timespan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has timespan</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links a Period authority to its temporal extent, represented as a
        Timespan node. Used for locally-defined periods or when more detailed
        temporal modeling is needed than provided by external period definitions.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Period"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_identifier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contributor identifier</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Additional persistent identifier for the contributor. Can be used
        for identifiers other than ORCID, such as:
        - ISNI (International Standard Name Identifier)
        - VIAF (Virtual International Authority File)
        - Wikidata QID (for persons or organizations)
        - Internal system identifiers

        When using this property, specify the identifier type in the value
        (e.g., "ISNI:0000000121032683" or "VIAF:12345678").
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#geo_json">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geometry GeoJSON</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        GeoJSON representation of the geometry. Used for web mapping
        applications and LPF export. Should be a valid GeoJSON geometry
        object (not a Feature or FeatureCollection).
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Attested">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#FormStatusScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">attested</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A form read in the cited source. The default form status, usually omitted.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#value_json">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">structured value</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A structured value serialised as JSON, whose shape is declared by
        plato:value_type. Use plato:value_literal instead for scalar values.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3060-0181">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Person"/>
    <foaf:name>Stephen Gadd</foaf:name>
    <foaf:account rdf:resource="https://github.com/docuracy"/>
    <foaf:mbox rdf:resource="mailto:stephen.gadd@pitt.edu"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#attests_type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attests type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to a Type classification it assigns to the SpatialEntity.
        An attestation may assert multiple types or no type at all.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Type"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#type_identifier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">type identifier</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">URI or identifier in a controlled vocabulary (e.g. AAT).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Type"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#notes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">notes</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Free-text notes providing additional context about this attestation.
        Can include transcriptions, translations, editorial comments, or
        other information that doesn't fit structured fields.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#member_of_group">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">member of group</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Links a Gazetteer to a GazetteerGroup.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#GazetteerGroup"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#LessCertain">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel"/>
    <plato:authority_title xml:lang="en">less certain</plato:authority_title>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The statement is probable but not certain.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Retracts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">retracts</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The target attestation is withdrawn: it no longer holds, and is kept only so that earlier states of the gazetteer can be reproduced. For a claim its maker takes back, such as a bad import, where plato:Contradicts would read as one scholar disputing another. A tool showing the current state leaves a retracted attestation out. A retraction is itself an attestation: retracting it withdraws the withdrawal, and the target holds again.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#period_periodo_uri">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PeriodO URI</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Link to a PeriodO period definition for this Period authority.
        When present, indicates this Period aligns with a standardized PeriodO
        definition. The PeriodO record provides authoritative temporal bounds,
        spatial extent, and cultural context. Example: 'https://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p06v8w4rb38'
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Period"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Beyond">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">beyond</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">On the far side of the anchor from the point of view of the source.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#start_latest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">start latest</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The latest possible start date for this timespan. For certain dates,
        this equals start_earliest. For uncertain dates, defines the end of
        the uncertainty range for when the interval began.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Literal"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#candidate_candidate">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">candidate entity</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Supports">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">supports</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Provides additional evidence for the target attestation.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#end_latest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">end latest</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The latest possible end date for this timespan. For certain dates,
        this equals end_earliest. For uncertain dates, defines the end of
        the uncertainty range for when the interval ended.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Literal"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contains_attestation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contains attestation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Links a Gazetteer to an Attestation contributed within it.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b8">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <owl:unionOf rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b9"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#promoted_from">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">promoted from</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an IdentityRelation to the Candidate from which it was
        promoted, preserving the algorithmic provenance. Optional:
        identity relations can also be created directly by human
        assertion without a prior Candidate.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b17">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Type"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b18"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#certainty">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">certainty</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Epistemic certainty, from 0.0 (completely uncertain) to 1.0
        (completely certain). On an Attestation it is the contributor's
        confidence in the bundle as a whole. On a facet node (Name, Geometry,
        Timespan, Type, PropertyValue) it is the confidence in that value
        alone, and may differ from the attestation's overall certainty.
        Certainty reflects the limits of evidence: better evidence could in
        principle raise it. Distinct from fuzziness (the referent has no
        sharp boundary) and from spatial_precision (how finely a location
        is specified). No rdfs:domain, so it can qualify any node.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_affiliation_string">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">affiliation (string)</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Free-text organizational affiliation when a structured Organization
        entity is not available. Example: "University of Pittsburgh,
        Institute for Spatial History Innovation"
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#attests_timespan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attests timespan</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to the Timespan during which the attested
        properties (name, geometry, type) were applicable. Distinct from
        the attestation's creation date: this is the historical timespan
        being asserted, not when the assertion was made.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#locator">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">locator</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Where in the cited work the evidence sits: 'p. 412', 'f. 12v', 'Table
        VI, col. 3, row 88', 's.v. Athlone', 'no. xix'. An uninterpreted
        string, deliberately: sources locate themselves inconsistently, and
        normalising the value would destroy exactly the information it is
        there to carry. The same position and shape as PeriodO's source
        locator.

        A property of this citation, not of the source: one Source for a
        printed gazetteer, a different locator on each attestation drawn
        from it. Not to be confused with the spatial sense of 'locator' in
        the plato:geometry_role comment, a proxy point standing in for an
        entity.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Citation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#authority_version">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">version</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Version identifier for tracking upstream authority updates.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#OccurrenceContextScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">occurrence context</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:occurrence_context. The capacity in which an attested form occurs in its source.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Geometry</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A spatial representation of a SpatialEntity's extent or location. Geometries
        are independent, reusable entities. The same Geometry may be referenced
        by multiple Attestations across different SpatialEntities or time periods.

        Note: GeometryCollection is not supported in some graph database
        implementations. For heterogeneous geometry sets (e.g., both point and
        polygon), use multiple Geometry entities linked through separate
        attestations.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#certainty_level">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">certainty level</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Certainty as a word rather than a number: a CertaintyLevel, such as
        plato:Certain, plato:LessCertain or plato:Uncertain (the three of Linked
        Places Format), or one a project declares. Use it when a source or a
        dataset states certainty in words, rather than inventing a number for
        plato:certainty; the two may also be given together. Like
        plato:certainty it has no rdfs:domain, so it can qualify an Attestation
        or any facet node.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_asserted_at">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">asserted at</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#property_label">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">property label</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Human-readable label for the property (e.g. 'population').</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#VariantOf">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">variant of</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A name defined as a variant of the anchor name.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Certainty Level</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A defined level of certainty for use in attestations, allowing
        projects to standardise their confidence vocabulary. An attestation or
        facet points at one with plato:certainty_level. PLATO declares the three
        levels of Linked Places Format (plato:Certain, plato:LessCertain,
        plato:Uncertain); a project may declare its own.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#DerivedFrom">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">derived from</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">This attestation's value was derived from the target attestation's, as a normalised search form is derived from an attested spelling.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#source_crs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">source CRS</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Original coordinate reference system. Typically 'EPSG:4326' (WGS84),
        but may reference historical or custom CRS identifiers for historical
        maps. Enables proper interpretation of coordinate values.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#source_label">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">source label</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The original label as given in the contributing source, before
        vocabulary mapping. For a structured PropertyValue this preserves the
        unparsed original — 'Monday after Whitsun, 3 days' — which is what a
        future reader will want to check against the document. On a Name it is
        the form as printed where the toponym has been normalised; on a
        Geometry, the coordinates as printed ('03-47S/13038E', '??'); on a
        Timespan, the date as written ('about 1841'). On an Attestation it is
        the source's own wording of the relation the attestation asserts
        ('part of Berkshire', 'within Buckingham'), before that relation is
        mapped to a RelationType; an attestation asserts at most one relation,
        so the wording is never ambiguous. Like the qualification properties it
        has no rdfs:domain, so it applies to any facet node or attestation.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionAccuracyScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">transcription accuracy</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:transcription_accuracy. How faithfully a transcribed value renders what the source shows.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b21">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b22"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Headword">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#FormStatusScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">headword</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The editorial or preferred form under which the cited authority files the entry. A scholarly decision rather than a reading.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relative_qualifier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">relative qualifier</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A controlled-vocabulary term describing the relative relationship
        when bearing and distance are not applicable or not sufficient.
        Should point to a concept in a SKOS concept scheme (e.g.
        plato:Near, plato:Within, plato:Beyond, plato:UpstreamOf,
        plato:BetweenXAndY, plato:DuringReignOf, plato:VariantOf).
        Using URIs rather than free text enables machine reconciliation
        across gazetteers that describe relativity differently.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b19"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#has_meta_type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">meta-attestation type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The nature of a meta-attestation's relationship to its target attestation.
        Should reference a controlled vocabulary concept defining the relationship
        type. The starter concepts, declared in this ontology's MetaTypeScheme:

        - plato:Contradicts: asserts the target attestation is incorrect
        - plato:Supports: provides additional evidence for the target attestation
        - plato:Supersedes: replaces the target attestation with newer information
        - plato:Refines: provides more precise information than the target
        - plato:Challenges: questions the target attestation's validity
        - plato:Bundles: groups the target with other related attestations
        - plato:DerivedFrom: this attestation's value was derived from the
          target's (a normalised search form from an attested spelling)
        - plato:Annotates: adds a remark about the target, with its own source,
          without supporting, contradicting or refining it; the remark is the
          meta-attestation's notes
        - plato:AlternativeTo: this attestation and the target are alternative
          readings of the same evidence, at most one of them right
        - plato:Retracts: the target attestation is withdrawn by whoever made
          it, and kept only so that earlier states can be reproduced

        Using SKOS concepts rather than strings enables vocabulary management
        and multilingual labels.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_orcid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ORCID</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        ORCID identifier for person contributors. Can be provided in two formats:

        1. Compact format: 0000-0000-0000-0000 (sixteen digits in groups of four)
        2. URI format: https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000 (preferred)

        The URI format (https://orcid.org/...) is preferred and required for
        proper linked data representation. ORCID is particular about always
        including the https:// protocol and domain prefix.

        While optional in the general ontology, specific implementations
        (e.g., WHG) may require ORCID for person contributors to ensure
        proper attribution and citation.

        ORCID provides persistent identification for researchers, enabling
        proper credit and reducing name ambiguity.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relation_range">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">range constraint</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Valid object type for this relation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#gazetteer_owner">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">owner</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionReconstructable">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionCompletenessScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">reconstructable</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Part of the value is lost, damaged or abbreviated, but can be supplied with confidence.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Within">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">within</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Inside the anchor.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#bibliographic_string">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bibliographic string</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A formatted citation string for this source or other authority. The
        domain is Authority, not Source, because any authority can be cited
        formally, a dataset as much as a document. Restricting it to Source
        made a cited Dataset a Source as well, which the disjoint union of
        Authority forbids.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_certainty">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identity certainty</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Confidence in the identity assertion, from 0.0 to 1.0. This is
        the epistemic certainty of the asserter, distinct from identity_type
        which describes the semantic nature of the relationship.
        A closeMatch can have high certainty ('I'm confident these are
        close but not exact') and an exactMatch can have low certainty
        ('I think they're identical but I'm not sure').
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#value_datatype">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">value datatype</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Datatype of the literal value (e.g. xsd:integer, xsd:decimal, xsd:string).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Near">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">near</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Close to the anchor, without a stated bearing or distance.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#start_precision">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">start precision</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Temporal granularity of the start bound. Values: 'year', 'decade',
        'century', 'era', 'geological_period'. This describes the resolution
        of the date specification, distinct from certainty (which describes
        the confidence in that specification).
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Agent"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Contributor</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A person, team, or automated process that creates attestations.
        Aligned with both foaf:Agent and prov:Agent for provenance
        chain-of-custody tracking.

        Contributors should be identified using persistent identifiers where
        possible. For persons, ORCID (https://orcid.org/) is strongly
        recommended and may be required by specific implementations. ORCID
        identifiers should use the full URI format (https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000)
        rather than just the numeric portion, as ORCID is particular about
        proper URI formatting for linked data interoperability.

        For organizations, ROR (https://ror.org/) or ISNI identifiers are
        recommended. Proper identification ensures accurate attribution,
        enables citation, and supports research assessment.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Itinerary">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#GeometryRoleScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">itinerary</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A line tracing a route or journey.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#attests_property">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attests property</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to a PropertyValue it asserts of the SpatialEntity — a
        population, a valuation, an elevation, a market's trading day. An
        attestation may assert several properties or none.

        These are among the commonest things a source says about an entity, and
        they carry exactly the provenance and temporal scoping the attestation
        pattern exists to handle: a population figure is a claim, by a named
        source, about one SpatialEntity, over a Timespan, with a certainty. The
        attestation's own timespans scope the property as they scope every
        other facet in the bundle.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#AttributionStatusScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attribution status</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:attribution_status. Whether the attribution of evidence to a cited source was stated or resolved editorially.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Extent">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#GeometryRoleScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">extent</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The built or bounded extent of the SpatialEntity.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#algorithm_version">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">algorithm version</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology"/>
    <owl:versionInfo>0.5.0</owl:versionInfo>
    <dcterms:title xml:lang="en">PLATO — Place Attestation Ontology</dcterms:title>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">
        An ontology for representing historical place knowledge as attestations:
        bundles of evidence linking SpatialEntities (settlements, routes,
        networks, administrative units, regions and other entities whose
        identity is bound up with space) to Names, Geometries, Timespans,
        Types, PropertyValues and Authorities with full provenance.

        Developed by the Pelagios Network's Place Working Group, led by the
        Institute for Spatial History Innovation (ISHI), University of Pittsburgh.

        PLATO formalises the WHG v4 data model, in which the Attestation is
        the fundamental unit of contributed knowledge. The Linked Places
        Format (LPF) is defined as its single-object-attestation profile:
        every LPF element corresponds to one attestation linking one entity
        to one name, type, geometry or related entity, so existing LPF files
        remain valid. The use cases of Linked Traces are accommodated within
        the same model.
    </dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3060-0181"/>
    <dcterms:contributor>Pelagios Network Place Working Group</dcterms:contributor>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <dcterms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-02-16</dcterms:created>
    <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://pelagios.org/place-attestation-ontology/index-en.html"/>
    <vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>plato</vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>
    <vann:preferredNamespaceUri>https://w3id.org/plato#</vann:preferredNamespaceUri>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#ProperInterval"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Timespan</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A temporal interval with start and end bounds. Timespans are
        independent, reusable entities that may be shared across multiple
        Attestations. Precision and uncertainty are expressed through
        the start and end bound properties. Aligned with W3C Time Ontology
        and compatible with PeriodO period definitions.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#precision_km">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">precision in kilometers</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Uncertainty radius in kilometers. For point geometries, indicates
        how far the true location might be from the stated coordinates.
        For polygons, indicates boundary uncertainty. Can be multiple values
        for heterogeneous geometries.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#derived_from">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">derived from</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        This Source is a copy, transcript, edition, calendar or confirmation
        of that one. A later cartulary copy of a charter, an inspeximus, or a
        printed edition of a manuscript is a Source of its own, with its own
        source_timespan, derived_from the document it transmits. An
        attestation should cite the Source it was actually read in; the chain
        of derived_from links records what that Source transmits. The kind of
        derivation (copy, edition, calendar, inspeximus) is not yet
        distinguished; a sub-property or a small vocabulary can be added when
        a project needs it.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Source"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Source"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Preferred">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#FormStatusScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">preferred</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The form the contributing project prefers for display and search: a project decision, so the cited source may show a different form.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#GazetteerGroup">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Gazetteer Group</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A thematic or organisational grouping of Gazetteers (e.g.
        'EMEW — Gazetteer of Early Modern England and Wales').
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#toponym">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#FunctionalProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">toponym</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The name string itself in its original script. This is the primary
        property of a Name entity. Required for all Name instances.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#attribution_status">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attribution status</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Whether the attribution of the evidence to the cited source was
        stated by the source or resolved editorially. Should point to a
        concept in a SKOS concept scheme. The starter concepts are:

        - plato:AttributionStated: the source is named where the form
          occurs. The default, and usually omitted.
        - plato:AttributionInferred: the source was given as 'ibidem' or
          'idem' and resolved by an editor, so the attribution is inferred
          rather than stated. Not marginal: about one attestation in twenty
          in the English Place-Name Society corpus.

        A statement about the citation, not about the form, which is why it
        lives here and not in plato:occurrence_context.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Citation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#publisher">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">publisher</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#licence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">licence</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#precision_value">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">precision value</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Numeric precision indicator in years. For example, a decade-level
        precision would have value 10, century-level would be 100. Enables
        computational comparison of precision across timespans.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contains_identity_relation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contains identity relation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links a Gazetteer to an IdentityRelation asserted within it. The
        submission profiles carry identity relations at the top level of a
        document, beside the entities and attestations, and this is the
        property that top level maps to.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#period_label">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">period label</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Human-readable label for this period (e.g., 'Byzantine period',
        'Han Dynasty', 'Early Bronze Age'). Distinct from authority_title,
        which may be more formal.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Period"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#Dataset"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Gazetteer</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A mutable, versioned workspace of SpatialEntities and their associated
        Attestations, owned by a contributor or team. Replaces the WHG v3
        concepts of Dataset, Dataset Collection, and Place Collection.
        A Gazetteer can be incrementally edited, enriched, and corrected
        without re-ingestion.

        As a dcat:Dataset, a Gazetteer is titled with dcterms:title and
        licensed with dcterms:license. Not with plato:authority_title or
        plato:licence: their domains are plato:Authority and plato:Dataset,
        so using them would make the Gazetteer one of the Authorities it
        contains attestations about.

        Versions, and so citations that reproduce. A SpatialEntity's evidence
        grows and is corrected while its identity stays the same, so a
        citation of it must say which state was meant. For the same reason
        as above, a Gazetteer's version uses DCAT 3's own properties, not a
        PLATO one: dcat:version for its version indicator, and, for a frozen
        snapshot published under its own IRI, dcat:isVersionOf (the living
        Gazetteer) and dcat:previousVersion (the snapshot before). A
        SpatialEntity is then cited as its IRI plus the Gazetteer version,
        one number per gazetteer rather than one per entity, as PeriodO cites.

        Once a Gazetteer's plato:gazetteer_status is 'published', its
        attestations are append-only, and this is normative: an attestation is
        never deleted or changed. A correction is a new attestation with a
        meta-attestation (plato:Supersedes, plato:Contradicts), and a
        withdrawal is a meta-attestation of type plato:Retracts. Each carries
        plato:created. The state as of any moment D is then a filter over the
        data: the attestations created by D, less those superseded or
        retracted by D. A supersession or retraction takes effect only while
        it holds itself, so retracting a retraction restores its target, and
        a chain of them resolves the same way; a cycle is an error in the
        data. A platform can offer that state on request, by
        datetime negotiation on the entity's IRI (RFC 7089, Memento); PLATO
        recommends this but does not require it, since it is behaviour of a
        server, not of data.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#GeometryRoleScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geometry role</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:geometry_role. What a geometry depicts, as distinct from how precisely it is known.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#fuzziness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">fuzziness</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Ontological fuzziness: a measure of how inherently vague or
        indeterminate the facet's referent is, from 0.0 (crisp, sharply
        bounded) to 1.0 (maximally vague). Distinct from certainty:
        fuzziness is a property of the thing itself, not of our knowledge
        of it. A regional boundary that was never precisely defined has
        high fuzziness regardless of how much evidence we have.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b23">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Type"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#periodo_uri">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PeriodO URI</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Link to a PeriodO period definition (https://perio.do/). When a timespan
        corresponds to a standardized period in PeriodO, this property enables
        alignment and allows PLATO to inherit PeriodO's temporal bounds, spatial
        coverage, and cultural context. Example: 'https://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p06v8w4rb38'
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#generated_at">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">generated at</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_asserted_by">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">asserted by</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The agent who made or confirmed this identity assertion.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#attests_about">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#FunctionalProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attests about</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to the SpatialEntity it is about. Every attestation must
        have exactly one subject entity. This is the primary entity being
        described by the bundled name, geometry, timespan, and type assertions.

        Functional property: each attestation has exactly one subject entity.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <owl:disjointUnionOf rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b1"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Authority</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Abstract superclass for all reference entities that provide provenance
        or semantic grounding for attestations. Subtypes include Source,
        Dataset, Period, RelationType, and CertaintyLevel.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Contradicts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">contradicts</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Asserts that the target attestation is incorrect.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Period">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Period</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A named historical period (e.g. 'Byzantine', 'Early Modern'),
        optionally linked to PeriodO or defined locally. Includes temporal
        bounds for period-based querying.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Dataset</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A collection-level authority: a published gazetteer, a contributed
        dataset, or an external authority corpus (GeoNames, Wikidata, TGN,
        OSM, Pleiades). Carries metadata such as title, version, publisher,
        licence, and URI.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#language">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">language</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        BCP 47 language tag for this name (e.g., 'ar' for Arabic, 'zh-Hans'
        for Simplified Chinese). Recommended: use ISO 639-3 three-letter codes
        within BCP 47 for better granularity (e.g., 'ara', 'cmn').
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#ContainedIn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType"/>
    <plato:authority_title xml:lang="en">contained in</plato:authority_title>
    <plato:relation_label>contained_in</plato:relation_label>
    <plato:inverse_label>contains</plato:inverse_label>
    <plato:relation_domain>SpatialEntity</plato:relation_domain>
    <plato:relation_range>SpatialEntity</plato:relation_range>
    <plato:authority_uri rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://vocab.getty.edu/ontology#broaderPartitive</plato:authority_uri>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The subject SpatialEntity is contained in the target SpatialEntity, for
        the attested timespan: a parish in a hundred, a hundred in a county.
        Not an identity relation and not fixed by the class model; it is a
        claim, with a source and a certainty, like any other attestation.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#AlternativeTo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">alternative to</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">This attestation and the target are alternative readings of the same evidence: at most one of them is right. Each keeps its own certainty. Link every alternative in a set to the same first one. A set of alternatives is one piece of evidence, not several, so it must be counted once.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b12">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Type"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relation_label">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">relation label</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Forward label for the relationship (e.g. 'capital_of').</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributed_by">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contributed by</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The agent (person, team, or process) who created this attestation.

        Semantically equivalent to PROV-O's prov:wasAttributedTo for provenance
        tracking. We don't use formal rdfs:subPropertyOf to avoid importing
        PROV-O's domain/range constraints. Distinct from sourced_by, which
        indicates the historical source being cited.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#occurrence_context">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">occurrence context</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The capacity in which the attested form occurs in the source. Should
        point to a concept in a SKOS concept scheme. The starter concepts are:

        - plato:Direct: the form refers to the SpatialEntity directly. The
          default, and usually omitted.
        - plato:InPersonalName: the form occurs only as an element of a
          personal name, a surname or by-name such as 'Willelmus de
          Bonestou'. This is the '(p)' of the place-name surveys, and it is
          weaker evidence for the name's currency, and for the entity's
          existence, than a direct attestation. Nothing else in the record
          distinguishes Bonestou the hundred from de Bonestou the man.
        - plato:InFieldName: the form survives only as an element of a later
          minor name, such as a field name.

        The editorially resolved 'ibidem' of the surveys is not an occurrence
        context. It is a statement about the citation, and belongs on the
        Citation as plato:attribution_status.

        Not a certainty. A '(p)' attestation may be entirely certain as an
        attestation; what differs is the kind of evidence it constitutes, and
        folding that into a lower plato:certainty would discard the reason.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#fuzziness_note">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">fuzziness note</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Free-text explanation of the nature of ontological fuzziness
        (e.g. 'regional boundary was never formally demarcated',
        'name used inconsistently across contemporary sources',
        'transition was gradual rather than a discrete event').
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relates_to">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">relates to</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to a second SpatialEntity, asserting a relationship
        between the Attestation's subject entity (via attests_about) and this
        target entity. The semantic nature of the relationship is specified by
        plato:has_relation_type, which references a RelationType authority.

        Example: An attestation about Constantinople that relates_to the
        Byzantine Empire with relation_type "capital_of" asserts that
        "Constantinople was the capital of the Byzantine Empire".
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b16">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b17"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Organization">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Organization</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        An organization to which a Contributor is affiliated. This is a
        lightweight class defined primarily to avoid ambiguity in ontology
        visualizations. Organizations can be further described using properties
        from FOAF, schema.org, or other ontologies.

        Organizations should use persistent identifiers where possible:
        - ROR (Research Organization Registry): https://ror.org/
        - ISNI (International Standard Name Identifier)
        - Wikidata QIDs
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Citation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Citation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        One attestation's use of one Authority: the qualified form of
        plato:sourced_by. Carries what is true of this citation rather than
        of the source or of the attestation: the locator within the work
        (page, folio, column, table cell, entry), and whether the attribution
        was stated in the source or inferred editorially. It carries no date.
        The date of a witness belongs on the Source (plato:source_timespan)
        and the date of the claim on the Attestation's Timespan.

        An attestation resting on several sources has several Citations,
        each with its own locator. Where there is nothing to say about the
        citation, plato:sourced_by alone is sufficient.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#attests_name">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attests name</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to a Name it asserts for the SpatialEntity. An attestation
        may assert multiple names (e.g., primary name and variants) or no name
        at all (e.g., geometry-only attestations).
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#transcription_completeness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">transcription completeness</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Whether a transcribed value is whole: a concept in
        plato:TranscriptionCompletenessScheme, plato:TranscriptionComplete,
        plato:TranscriptionReconstructable (part is lost but can be supplied
        with confidence) or plato:TranscriptionNonReconstructable (part is lost
        and cannot be supplied). A different axis from accuracy: a damaged form
        can be accurately read as far as it goes. No rdfs:domain, as for
        accuracy.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionNonReconstructable">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionCompletenessScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">not reconstructable</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Part of the value is lost and cannot be supplied.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b22">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b23"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#similarity_score">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">similarity score</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#sourced_by">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">sourced by</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links an Attestation to the Authority (typically a Source or Dataset)
        that grounds it. This provides the provenance chain for the attestation.

        Semantically equivalent to PROV-O's prov:wasDerivedFrom, indicating the
        authority is the origin of the attested knowledge. We don't use formal
        rdfs:subPropertyOf to avoid importing PROV-O's domain/range constraints
        which conflict with PLATO's class structure.

        This is the direct form. Where the citation itself has something to
        say (a page or folio, an attribution resolved from 'ibidem'), use
        plato:has_citation to a Citation node that cites the Authority.
        sourced_by is declared as the property chain has_citation o cites, so
        a reasoner derives it from a Citation; data intended for consumers
        without a reasoner should assert it directly as well.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <owl:propertyChainAxiom rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b6"/>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#FeaturePoint">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#GeometryRoleScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">feature point</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The location of a specific central feature of the SpatialEntity, such as a market place or a church.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b7">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#cites"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#property_type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">property type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        URI of the property in an external vocabulary — a Wikidata property,
        a CIDOC-CRM property, or a project vocabulary term (e.g.
        &lt;https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Property:P1082&gt; for population).
        Keeping the vocabulary external rather than enumerating attributes in
        this ontology is what allows the model to carry attributes it has
        never heard of.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#authority_title">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">title</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#AttributionStated">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#AttributionStatusScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">attribution stated</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The source is named where the form occurs. The default attribution status, usually omitted.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_basis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identity basis</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Free-text explanation of the evidence or reasoning supporting
        this identity assertion (e.g. 'coordinates within 500m and
        name forms are cognate', 'identified by contributor during
        reconciliation review', 'confirmed via TGN cross-reference').
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#AttributionInferred">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#AttributionStatusScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">attribution inferred</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The source was given as ibidem or idem and resolved by an editor, so the attribution is inferred rather than stated.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Entity"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Spatial Entity</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        An entity whose identity is bound up with space, whether its spatial
        extent is attested directly, applies notionally (as with the region to
        which a period applies), or is derived from its constituents (as with a
        collection). A SpatialEntity need not have any attested geometry: lost,
        imagined and unlocated entities are admissible.

        What kind of entity it is (settlement, route, region, administrative
        unit, period and so on) is not fixed by the class. It is established by
        Type attestations, so a classification is a claim rather than an axiom.

        SpatialEntities are not defined by any single attestation. They are the
        point of convergence for many attestations from different sources,
        periods and contributors.

        PLATO defines no Place class and gives "place" no prescribed
        meaning. Many SpatialEntities are what people would call places and
        some are not, and the word is used differently across languages and
        disciplines. The ontology therefore neither equates a SpatialEntity
        with a place nor defines a place in terms of SpatialEntities or
        their attestations. Where the word appears in this document it is
        used in its ordinary sense.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#ipa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">IPA</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        International Phonetic Alphabet representation of the name's
        pronunciation. Enables phonetic search and comparison across scripts
        and languages.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b19">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <owl:unionOf rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b20"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#repr_point">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">representative point</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A single representative point (centroid, label point, or best
        estimate) for spatial indexing and distance queries. Stored as WKT
        Point. Required for all geometries to enable spatial search.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contains_entity">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contains entity</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Links a Gazetteer to a SpatialEntity it contains.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#open_end">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">open end</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Boolean flag indicating that the end of this interval is deliberately
        unspecified (not merely unknown). True for ongoing periods like 'Modern
        era (1800-present)' or situations where the end does not exist. Aligns
        with PeriodO's OpenEndInterval.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relative_to">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">relative to</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Links a facet node to its anchor entity, indicating that this
        facet is defined relationally rather than absolutely. The anchor
        is typically a SpatialEntity, Period, or another facet (Name, Timespan, etc.).

        Common patterns:
        - Spatial relativity: "20km east of Nanjing" (anchor is a SpatialEntity)
        - Temporal relativity: "during the reign of Justinian" (anchor is a Period)
        - Name relativity: "also known as Rome" (anchor is another Name)
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b8"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b13"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#partOf">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">part of</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Links a Source to its parent Dataset.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Source"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#connection_metadata">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">connection metadata</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Structured metadata for network-type attestations. Can encode
        information about trade goods, flow direction, capacity, frequency,
        or other properties of network connections. Format is implementation-
        specific (JSON recommended) but should be documented in the gazetteer's
        metadata.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionComplete">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionCompletenessScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">complete</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Nothing of the value is lost.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Property Value</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A quantitative or descriptive attribute of a SpatialEntity, expressed as a
        property drawn from an external vocabulary together with its value.
        Covers the many things a historical source says that are neither a
        name, a location, a period nor a classification — populations,
        valuations, elevations, recurrence rules for markets and fairs.

        The property itself is identified by URI rather than enumerated here,
        so the vocabulary stays open and delegated (Wikidata properties,
        CIDOC-CRM, project vocabularies), in the same way that Type delegates
        its classifications to AAT.

        Values may be literals (with a datatype and optional unit) or
        structured objects whose shape is declared by plato:value_type. As a
        facet node, a PropertyValue may carry the qualification properties
        (certainty, fuzziness) like any other.

        Where the value is another SpatialEntity, use an entity-to-entity
        relation instead; this class is for attributes of the entity. A person
        or organisation connected to it (the grantee of a market right, an
        owner, a lord of the manor) is such an attribute: the value is the
        agent's IRI, ideally in an authority such as Wikidata or VIAF, with
        value_datatype xsd:anyURI, and the property names the role, for
        example http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P127 ('owned by'). This
        asserts nothing false, and needs no agent class: PLATO's own agent
        classes (Contributor, Organization) describe who recorded the data.

        A figure from a statistical table (a census count, a toll receipt) is
        a PropertyValue that is also a qb:Observation of the W3C RDF Data
        Cube vocabulary. It carries qb:dataSet (the table, a qb:DataSet), one
        direct statement for each of its coordinates with the table's
        qb:DimensionProperty predicates, one for each fact about the figure
        with qb:AttributeProperty predicates (sdmx-attribute:obsStatus for a
        cell's state), and plato:universe for the figure it is a part or share
        of. So the county is not said to have 'class 1': the observation is.
        The measure stays plato:property_type and plato:value_literal, and the
        area and date stay on the attestation; a cube export adds the direct
        measure statement, sdmx-dimension:refArea and refPeriod. A printed dash
        is an observation with an obsStatus and no value, never 0. Money is
        an exact integer in the smallest unit of its system of account
        (farthings, for sterling amounts with halfpennies), the unit an IRI
        from a fitting authority or minted by the encoding project, ideally
        naming a unit of account in a named system ('penny sterling'); PLATO
        mints no currency and converts nothing. A coordinate the source does
        not give is an explicit 'unknown' code. A table mixing measures is one
        qb:DataSet per measure. Dimension values are concepts, with the
        source's label as skos:prefLabel, in code lists the encoding project
        publishes where none exists. A statistical table in spreadsheet form
        keeps its own CSV on the Web description and links to places by
        place_id. The design, and its testing on three corpora, is issue #14.

        A latitude given without a longitude (a source's 'at 9.5 degrees
        north') is a PropertyValue, not a Geometry, since it is not a location:
        property http://www.w3.org/2003/01/geo/wgs84_pos#lat, the value in
        decimal degrees. A longitude alone is the same, with wgs84_pos#long.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#namespace">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">namespace</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Whose identifier plato:entity_identifier is (e.g. 'geonames', 'wikidata',
        'tgn', 'osm', 'pleiades', 'whg', or the contributing project's own short
        name). Authority-sourced SpatialEntities carry their origin namespace;
        contributed SpatialEntities that cannot be linked to any authority
        receive the 'whg' namespace.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#start_earliest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">start earliest</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The earliest possible start date for this timespan. Together with
        start_latest, defines the uncertainty range for when the interval began.
        Use ISO 8601 format (e.g., '1453-05-29') or a year of at least four
        digits ('0921', '-12000') for year-level precision; deep-time years
        have as many digits as they need, as in xsd:gYear. May be null for unknown or deliberately
        open-beginning intervals.

        The range is rdfs:Literal rather than a single datatype, because one
        property carries values of three shapes. A plain string is conformant;
        a producer that can type by shape should emit xsd:gYear for a year,
        xsd:date for a day and xsd:dateTime otherwise, so that a store can
        index and compare the bounds. The same applies to start_latest,
        end_earliest and end_latest.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Literal"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#romanized">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">romanized</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Romanized (Latin-script) form of the name for searchability and
        display in contexts requiring ASCII. Should specify transliteration
        system used via plato:transliteration_system.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#negated">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">negated</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        True when the source states that what this attestation bundles is not
        so: that a place had no market, that a name was not used, that a grant
        was refused. The attestation is still about a real SpatialEntity (the
        town that had no market), with the facet the source denies (a Type
        'market'); nothing that never existed need be invented. A denial is a
        statement a source makes, often a certain one, so it is neither low
        certainty nor a meta-attestation, which needs a claim to contradict.
        The 1886 return of market rights answered for every borough and
        district in England and Wales, and 1,000 of its 1,813 rows are denials.

        A consumer that does not implement this property will read a denial
        as an assertion, the opposite of what the source says. So it must be
        honoured: a tool that cannot express a denial in its target format
        must leave the attestation out and report it, never write it as an
        assertion. Bundle one denied facet per attestation, so that what is
        denied is never in doubt. Absent or false, the attestation asserts.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b3">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Period"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b4"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#modified">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">modified</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Timestamp when this attestation was last modified, while its
        Gazetteer is still a draft. Once the Gazetteer is published its
        attestations are append-only (see plato:Gazetteer): a published
        attestation is not modified, and a change is a new attestation with a
        meta-attestation, so that the state at any earlier moment can be
        recomputed and every citation of it reproduces.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#RepresentativePoint">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#GeometryRoleScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">representative point</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A proxy point standing in for the whole SpatialEntity, for indexing and display; it need not depict any feature of it.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Annotates">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">annotates</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Adds a remark about the target attestation, with its own source, without supporting, contradicting or refining it. The remark is the meta-attestation's notes.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#sequence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">sequence</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Ordering index for attestations in routes or itineraries. Defines
        the position of this attestation in an ordered sequence. Used when
        the SpatialEntity being attested (via attests_about) is a Route or Itinerary.
        Lower numbers come first in the sequence.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Relation Type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A vocabulary entry defining a semantic relationship between SpatialEntities:
        'capital_of', 'successor_to', 'connected_by_trade_route_to', etc.
        Carries a label, an inverse label, and domain/range constraints.
        This allows the vocabulary of historical relationships to grow
        without modifying the core ontology.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Certain">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel"/>
    <plato:authority_title xml:lang="en">certain</plato:authority_title>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The statement is certain: the source, or the contributor, has no doubt of it.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_affiliation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">affiliation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Organizational affiliation of the contributor. Links to a
        plato:Organization entity, which can be further described using
        FOAF, schema.org, or other ontology properties. Organizations
        should ideally use persistent identifiers (ROR, ISNI, etc.).

        For simple cases, use contributor_affiliation_string for free-text
        affiliation names.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Organization"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#candidate_source">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">source entity</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#end_earliest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">end earliest</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The earliest possible end date for this timespan. Together with
        end_latest, defines the uncertainty range for when the interval ended.
        May be null for unknown or deliberately open-end intervals.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Literal"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#relative_bearing">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bearing</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Compass bearing from the anchor, in degrees (0–360).
        Primarily used for relative geometries: '90' means east of
        the anchor. May carry its own certainty or fuzziness.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#citation_function">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">citation function</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Why the source is cited: as the evidence for the attestation, as a
        source of its data, as related reading, and so on. The value is a
        property of CiTO, the Citation Typing Ontology
        (http://purl.org/spar/cito/): cito:citesAsEvidence,
        cito:citesAsDataSource, cito:citesAsRelated, cito:citesForInformation,
        cito:citesAsRecommendedReading, or another of the properties below
        cito:cites. Only CiTO properties are used, so that every consumer reads
        the same function the same way; a project whose own vocabulary has
        other terms maps them to CiTO when it organises its data.

        Without it, 'this source is the evidence' and 'see this source for
        more' are the same link, which is the distinction an attestation model
        exists to make: 143,418 of Pleiades' 244,759 references are 'see
        further', and only 21,652 are cited as evidence. Absent, the function
        is unstated, as before; a consumer should not assume evidence.

        Only a property CiTO defines is a valid value. An IRI in the CiTO
        namespace is not enough: Pleiades writes cito:seeFurther and
        cito:seeAlso, which CiTO does not define, so the JSON Schema and the
        tables list the accepted properties exactly.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Citation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#geometry_role">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geometry role</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        What this geometry depicts, as distinct from how precisely it is known
        (plato:spatial_precision) or where its indexing point falls
        (plato:repr_point). A SpatialEntity may be attested with several geometries at
        once — a built extent, the location of a specific central feature, a
        proxy standing in for the whole, the anchor of a map label — and without
        a declared role a consumer cannot tell them apart, nor state which one
        an analysis used.

        Should point to a concept in a SKOS concept scheme (e.g. plato:Extent,
        plato:FeaturePoint, plato:RepresentativePoint, plato:LabelAnchor,
        plato:Itinerary). Using URIs rather than free text lets gazetteers that
        record geometry differently be compared.

        Note that role is orthogonal to precision: a proxy locator may be
        surveyed to the metre and still not depict the SpatialEntity it stands for. A
        parish church's coordinates contributed as a locator for a town are a
        representative point of high precision, not an imprecise feature point.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Uncertain">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel"/>
    <plato:authority_title xml:lang="en">uncertain</plato:authority_title>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The statement is doubtful: possible, but not established.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#inverse_label">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">inverse label</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Inverse label (e.g. 'has_capital').</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#geo_wkt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#asWKT"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geometry WKT</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Well-Known Text representation of the geometry, following OGC/ISO
        standards. Used for GeoSPARQL queries and RDF serialization.
        Supports Point, LineString, Polygon, MultiPoint, MultiLineString,
        MultiPolygon.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Identity Relation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        An assertion that two SpatialEntities refer to the same real-world entity.
        Carries its own provenance (who asserted it, on what basis),
        certainty (how confident the assertion is), and identity semantics
        (exactMatch, closeMatch, related).

        Identity relations are distinct from scholarly entity-to-entity
        relations, which describe substantive historical relationships
        between different entities. They are also distinct from Candidates,
        which are algorithm-generated candidates not yet confirmed by
        human review.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#identity_sourced_by">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identity sourced by</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The Authority (Source or Dataset) that provides evidence for
        this identity assertion, if any.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Bundles">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">bundles</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Groups the target with other related attestations.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Refines">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">refines</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Provides more precise information than the target attestation.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Challenges">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">challenges</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Questions the validity of the target attestation.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#edtf_string">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">EDTF string</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Extended Date/Time Format (EDTF) representation of this timespan.
        Provides standardized string encoding for interchange with systems
        using EDTF. Optional but recommended for interoperability with library
        and archival systems. Example: '1453-05-29/1453-05-29' or '1450~/1453~'
        for approximate dates.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#transliteration_system">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">transliteration system</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The transliteration/romanization system used (e.g., 'Pinyin' for
        Chinese, 'BGN/PCGN' for Arabic, 'ISO 259' for Hebrew, 'Hepburn' for
        Japanese). Enables proper interpretation and comparison of romanized
        forms.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#occurrence_count">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">occurrence count</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The number of times the attested form occurs in the cited source or
        passage: the '(3 X)' of a place-name survey. A count of occurrences
        of the form in this source, not of independent sources. It belongs to
        the attestation, not to the Name, which may occur elsewhere.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b18">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Period"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#DuringReignOf">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">during reign of</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A timespan defined by the reign or tenure of the anchor.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#name_type">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">name type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        What the name denotes. The list is open: 'toponym' (place name),
        'chrononym' (period or era name), 'ethnonym' (people or ethnic
        group), 'demonym' (inhabitants), 'odonym' (street or road name) and
        'hydronym' (water body) are the starter values, and a project may
        add others ('oronym', 'hagionym') provided it documents them in its
        gazetteer metadata. Multiple types are possible (a name that
        functions as both toponym and ethnonym). plato.schema.json carries
        the same open list; earlier releases closed it there, and the two
        serialisations now agree.

        This classifies the referent, not the status of the form. Whether a
        string is an attested spelling, an editorial headword or a derived
        search form is a property of the attestation (plato:form_status),
        because the same string can have a different status in different
        attestations.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_role">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contributor role</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Role of the contributor in the project or gazetteer. Examples:
        'principal_investigator', 'data_curator', 'student_researcher',
        'automated_process', 'external_contributor'. Helps understand the
        context and authority of contributions.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Name">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Name</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A toponym or appellation for a SpatialEntity. Names are independent, reusable
        entities. The association between a Name and a SpatialEntity is always mediated
        by an Attestation, which provides temporal scope and provenance.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_url">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contributor URL</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Website or profile URL for the contributor (personal website,
        institutional page, project page, GitHub profile, etc.). Provides
        additional context about the contributor's work and identity.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#LabelAnchor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#GeometryRoleScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">label anchor</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The anchor point of a map label.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b15">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b16"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b13">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <owl:unionOf rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b14"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#open_start">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">open start</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Boolean flag indicating that the start of this interval is deliberately
        unspecified (not merely unknown). True for intervals like 'everything
        before 1453' or 'prehistory until...'. Distinct from null start_earliest,
        which indicates unknown start. Aligns with PeriodO's OpenBeginningInterval.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b9">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b10"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b14">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b15"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionAccurate">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionAccuracyScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">accurate</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The transcription renders what the source shows.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">meta-attestation type</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:has_meta_type. The nature of a meta-attestation's relationship to its target attestation.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_name">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contributor name</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The name of the contributor (person, team, or automated process).
        For persons, should be the full name as preferred by the contributor.
        For teams, the team name. For automated processes, a descriptive name.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Normalised">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#FormStatusScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">normalised</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A form derived from an attested one for matching or search, attested by no source and carrying no independent evidential value.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Candidate</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        An algorithm-generated match candidate between two SpatialEntities, produced
        by the PLACE clustering engine. Candidates carry a similarity score,
        algorithm version, and review status. They are explicitly not
        IdentityRelations — they become IdentityRelations only when confirmed
        by a human reviewer (linked back via plato:promoted_from).
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#cites">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#FunctionalProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cites</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        The Authority (a Source or a Dataset) this Citation uses. Exactly one
        per Citation: an attestation resting on two sources has two
        Citations.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Citation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Authority"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#InFieldName">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#OccurrenceContextScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">in field name</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The form survives only as an element of a later minor name, such as a field name.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Reconstructed">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#FormStatusScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">reconstructed</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A form inferred but not attested; the philologists' asterisked form.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#spatial_coverage">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">spatial coverage</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Geographic or cultural region to which this period applies.
        Free text or URI to a place/region. Useful because periods like
        'Medieval' have different temporal bounds in different regions.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Period"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#timespan_label">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">timespan label</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Human-readable label for this timespan (e.g., 'Byzantine period',
        'Early Bronze Age', 'Ming Dynasty'). Particularly useful for named
        periods referenced across multiple attestations. The date exactly as a
        source writes it ('c. 925', 'a. 1300') is plato:source_label.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b11">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b12"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Entity"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Attestation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        A bundle of evidence asserting that a particular SpatialEntity had a particular
        Name, Geometry, Type, or combination thereof, during a particular
        Timespan, according to a particular Authority. The Attestation node
        carries only metadata; its semantic content is defined entirely by its
        outgoing relationships.

        IMPORTANT: Attestations are document nodes in the graph, not edges.
        They are instances of plato:Attestation (a class), not instances of
        owl:ObjectProperty. This design enables meta-attestations and full
        provenance tracking.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">relative qualifier</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:relative_qualifier. How a facet is defined relative to its anchor when bearing and distance are not applicable or not sufficient.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b4">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b5"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#gazetteer_status">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">status</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Publication status: 'draft' or 'published'. From 'published' on, the Gazetteer's attestations are append-only (see plato:Gazetteer).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionInaccurate">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionAccuracyScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">inaccurate</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The transcription departs from what the source shows, by misreading or error, but is a reading of something the source contains.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#created">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">created</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        Timestamp when this attestation was created. This is the contribution
        date, not the historical timespan being attested (use attests_timespan
        for that). In a published Gazetteer it is the time axis along which an
        earlier state is recomputed, so every attestation and meta-attestation
        there should carry it.

        Semantically equivalent to PROV-O's prov:generatedAtTime. We don't use
        formal rdfs:subPropertyOf to avoid importing PROV-O constraints.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b2">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b3"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionCompletenessScheme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">transcription completeness</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Starter concepts for plato:transcription_completeness. Whether a transcribed value is whole, and if not, whether the lost part can be supplied.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/plato#script">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">script</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">
        ISO 15924 script code (e.g., 'Arab' for Arabic script, 'Latn' for
        Latin script, 'Hant' for Traditional Han characters). Important for
        names that may appear in multiple scripts.
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b20">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#Name"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b21"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b6">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/plato#has_citation"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b7"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="n5eeffcd8f9b94513bc1396cbc454d2f3b1">
